  "query_preview": "..\nTonight: The robust area low pressure system responsible for \nthe last 12+ hours of rainfall will begin its trek eastward out\nof the region. Rain will diminish from west to east clearing \nout of Central WA this evening and linger across the Idaho \nPanhandle until early Monday morning. Rainfall amounts in the \nlast 24 hours have been quite impressive for southern Chelan \nCounty (1-2 inches) and 0.50-1.00 inches for many communities \nacross northern and northeastern WA including the Spokane Area. \nAs clouds clear out, I anticipate areas of fog to settle into \nareas where winds remain light. Locally breezy conditions will \ncontinue across southeastern which will favor low clouds vs fog \nincluding Pullman and Spokane area.\n\nMonday-Tuesday: Monday will bring a break in the precipitation \nwith light winds but continued cooler than normal temperatures \nwith the region under the influence of a broad upper-level \ntrough. A reinforcing shortwave will drop into the trough from \nthe north on Tuesday. This wave has trended stronger and \nstronger over the last few days bringing a 30-60% chance for \nshowers across the northern mountains and down the spine of the \nCascades. Rainfall amounts will be lighter and largely between a\nfew hundredths to a tenth with locally around a quarter of an \ninch on the peaks near the International Border. Shifting winds \nare expected as the wave progresses through the region starting \noff from the southwest Monday then shifting to the east Tuesday \nmorning and north to northeast Tuesday afternoon and night. \nLocally breezy conditions will be possible through the Okanogan \nValley and Waterville Plateau as winds shift to the north. \n\nWednesday-Sunday: The upper-low will dig into N CA/southern OR \nand continue to spin there for a few days before ejecting to \nthe east. Meanwhile, an upper-level ridge will expand into the \nregion from the Ern Pac with heights rising along the \nInternational border. This typically leads to low confidence \nwhere these cutoff lows will drift and uncertainty how quickly \nit will depart the west. This pattern also suggest a trend \ntoward drier conditions for much of the Inland NW. The exception\nwill be far SE WA and the lower ID Panhandle with potential for\nmoisture over the Great Plains to get pulled westward across \nEastern Oregon and skirt the far southeastern portion of the \nforecast area. Otherwise, temperatures will rebound into the 70s\nWednesday then 70-80s Thursday into the weekend. Over 50% of \nthe ensembles hint at a Pacific trough influencing the region by\nearly next week. /sb",
